{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the last ruler of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Middle East that existed from 1104 to 1289. He was born in 1135 and became the Count of Tripoli in 1160. Raymond III was a member of the Anjou dynasty and was the eldest son of Pons II Count of Tripoli and Gersende of the County of Tripoli. \n\n*Highlighted Section Part 1 - Reign*\n\nRaymond III succeeded his father on the throne in 1160 after the death of his father in 1152. He was 25 years old at the time of his accession. He became involved in several disputes with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tyre due to border disputes. Raymond III also faced opposition from the Mamluk Sultanate in Egypt and the Ayyubid Sultanate in Syria. \n\n*Highlighted Section Part 2 - Military Campaigns*\n\nRaymond III led several military campaigns including the Siege of Hypsipylus in 1171 and the Siege of Beirut in 1172. He also led a campaign against the Kingdom of Jerusalem in 1179. In 1187 Raymond III allied himself with Saladin the Sultan of Egypt and the Ayyubid Sultanate in an attempt to counter the growing power of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. However the alliance ultimately proved unsuccessful and Raymond III lost control of the County of Tripoli to Saladin's forces. \n\n*Highlighted Section Part 3 - Legacy*\n\nRaymond III died in 1190 while defending the County of Tripoli against Saladin's forces. He was killed in the Battle of Hittin. His legacy is that of a skilled military leader who fought to defend the County of Tripoli against the growing power of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Mamluk Sultanate. Despite his failure to preserve the County of Tripoli he is remembered as one of the most important rulers of the County.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.